Third-party notice — vendored ISF filters
=========================================

The nine `.fs` files in this directory are vendored VERBATIM, byte for byte,
from the Vidvox ISF shader collection:

    https://github.com/Vidvox/ISF-Files        (path: ISF/<name>.fs)
    Licence: MIT — the full text is in ./LICENSE, copied from that repository.

    Bad TV.fs                 CREDIT: by VIDVOX
                              Adapted upstream from Felix Turner's BadTVShader;
                              that author's own MIT notice is inside the file
                              and travels with it. Do not strip it.
    Broken LCD.fs              CREDIT: VIDVOX
    Bump Distortion.fs         CREDIT: by carter rosenberg
    CMYK Halftone.fs           CREDIT: by zoidberg
    Color Invert.fs            CREDIT: by zoidberg
    Dither-Bayer.fs             CREDIT: Hugh Kennedy, adapted by David Lublin
    JPEG Block Corruption.fs   CREDIT: by VIDVOX
    Pixel Shifter.fs           CREDIT: by VIDVOX
    Ripples.fs                 CREDIT: by carter rosenberg

Not edited. A vendored fixture is only worth having if it is what upstream
actually ships — the day one of these is "tidied up" it stops proving that the
adapter reads the real format and starts proving that it reads our idea of it.
Filenames keep their upstream spelling, spaces included, so the mapping back to
`ISF/<name>.fs` is exact rather than approximate.

WHY THE FIRST FIVE
-------------------
Each exercises something the adapter has to get right, and between them they
cover every ISF image function:

    Bad TV            IMG_NORM_PIXEL, TIME-driven animation, nine float inputs
    Broken LCD        IMG_PIXEL (with nested-paren arguments), IMG_THIS_PIXEL,
                      IMG_NORM_PIXEL, a `long` pop-up menu input
                      *** DOES NOT RUN — see UNUSABLE_FILTERS / F185. It still
                      earns its place here as the adapter fixture it was picked
                      to be, and it is still credited above; it is the SHADER
                      that is not portable, not the import of it. Its noise
                      sums go negative into pow(), undefined for a negative
                      base in GLSL, and the NaN blacks out the frame. ***
    Bump Distortion   IMG_NORM_PIXEL, point2D input, and the one body in the set
                      that writes a non-source alpha (`vec4(0.0)` out of bounds)
    CMYK Halftone     IMG_PIXEL, gl_FragCoord in pixel space, a const array
    Color Invert      IMG_THIS_PIXEL, and the smallest possible whole filter

Note what that list is and is not. Every one of the five was chosen because it
exercises the PARSER — none was chosen for how it looks, and nothing here was
ever screened for whether it renders well at its shipped defaults. Those are
two different reviews, and for a long time only the first had been run: that is
exactly how `Broken LCD` reached the live roster and blacked out a show (F185).
A file earning its place as an adapter fixture says nothing about whether it
should be offered to a viewer.

WHY THE NEXT FOUR
------------------
Added once the pipeline below (roster, budget, trigger, attribution UI) was
proven end to end against the first five — not before, for the reason the
"shippable" section used to warn about: curating and vendoring the corpus
before the pipeline exists just moves the F01 risk instead of avoiding it.
Screened for genuine visual distinctiveness against both the mounted post
chain (Bloom/ChromaticAberration/Vignette/GradePass/MirrorPass/FeedbackPass)
and the first five, and for a NON-DEGENERATE author DEFAULT — this engine has
no per-filter parameter UI yet, so a filter's `INPUTS` render at whatever the
header's own `DEFAULT` says, and a filter whose default is a no-op (checked:
`Noise Displace.fs`'s `displaceX`/`displaceY` both default to 0, making it
invisible until someone drives its parameters — passed over for exactly this
reason) would silently cost a full pass and show nothing:

    Dither-Bayer            ordered/Bayer dithering — retro halftone texture,
                            a `long` matrix-size menu, distinct from CMYK
                            Halftone's four-plate print-dot look
    JPEG Block Corruption   compression-artifact blocking/chroma-bleed — a
                            distinct flavour of "broken" from Bad TV/Broken
                            LCD's noise-based interference
    Pixel Shifter           row/column pixel-shift glitch — a third, distinct
                            glitch texture; scanline-shift rather than noise
                            or corruption
    Ripples                 concentric ripple/wave distortion from a fixed
                            centre — distinct from Bump Distortion's static
                            radial bulge (the same author's other filter
                            already vendored here); reuses the identical
                            `#ifndef GL_ES`-guarded `distance()` redefinition
                            pattern already proven safe by Bump Distortion

All four confirmed against the real adapter (`compileIsfFilter`, not just the
header) before vendoring: single `inputImage`, no `PASSES`/`IMPORTED`/audio
input/second image, no `FILTER_RESERVED_SYMBOLS` collision. Rejected on sight
for the same reasons: `Crosshatch`/`Glitch Displace`/`Mosaic`/`Pixelize`/
`Water Drop` (gl-transitions ports — `startImage`/`endImage`, the adapter's
Track D, not this one), `Interlace`/`Vertical Tearing` (`PASSES` — persistent
buffers this engine has no render-target allocator for), `v002-CRT-Mask`
(`IMPORTED` — external PNG textures), `Waveform Displace` (an `audio` input,
refused unconditionally, on top of `PASSES`), `Displace` (a second image
input, `displaceImage`).

Shipped and selectable
-----------------------
Unlike the state this file used to describe, these ARE wired into production:
`src/engine/isfFilterRoster.ts` compiles all nine and curates which are
selectable, `src/engine/FilterDirector.tsx` picks and times one on musical
events, `src/engine/PostFXChain.tsx` drives `IsfFilterPass` from that, and
`src/ui/Credits.tsx` (`I` key / the HUD's "ⓘ" chip) is the attribution
surface MIT requires — every filter here appears there with its credit,
whether or not it is currently in the selectable pool. See F178 in
docs/ISSUES.md for how that gate got answered.
